.TH SEMSET 2
.SH NAME
semset, semclear, semtest, semtset \- manipulate local/global binary semaphores
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "semset(sem, pri)"
.B "semclear(sem)"
.B "semtest(sem)"
.B "semtset(sem, pri)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Semaphores allow cooperating processes to ``lock out'' each other during
the execution of ``critical code'' regions, such as during updates to
shared data segments or any common data base.
.PP
.B semset
sets the semaphore
.I sem
if it was clear and returns to the caller;
otherwise it queues the calling process at priority
.I pri
with all other
processes waiting on
.I sem
and goes to sleep.
The values of
.I pri
may range
between 0, the highest priority, and 15, the lowest priority.
.PP
.B semclear
clears the semaphore previously set and wakes up the highest
priority process waiting on
.IR sem .
.PP
.B semtest
tests the semaphore and returns a zero if clear, a one if set.
If
clear, this does not guarantee that a subsequent
.B semset
will not have to
wait, since another process can do a
.B semset
in the intervening time.
.PP
.B semtset
tests the semaphore and returns a 1 if set.
If clear, then the semaphore
is set and a 0 is returned.
.PP
Semaphores can range in value between \-16 and 15.
The negative
values (\-16 to \-1) are global and the same for processes on the system,
while the positive values (0 to 15) are local and shared by all processes in
the same process group.
.PP
When a process forks, only the parent maintains the semaphore.
Semaphores
are maintained beyond the life of any program using them; they
are not cleared when a program exits.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A semaphore out of range or attempted setting if already set by the caller
is considered an error and a \-1 is returned.
.SH BUGS
If a process is swapped out while waiting on a semaphore, its priority is
ineffective for waking up.
.SH NOTES
Semaphores are not portable to standard UNIX.
.SH ASSEMBLER
(sema = 45.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
R0 = 0 (set), 1 (clear), 2 (test), or 3 (test & set)
sys sema; semaphore; priority
.fi
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 45
AX = 0 (set), 1 (clear), 2 (test), or 3 (test & set)
DX = semaphore
CX = priority
int 0Xf1
.fi
.sp
